 "productionNotes": "No source consulted for this draft publishes a production figure for the 3100 half-ton, for any single model year or for the run as a whole, and none publishes one for the 3600 or 3800. Truck production in this era is generally reported by calendar year across Chevrolet's whole truck line, and a line total cannot be split into half-tons by inference, so this page gives no 3100 number. What the sources do say is relative. Don Bunn's history of the trucks, reprinted on Stovebolt and on a second enthusiast site, states that Chevrolet pickups were number one in sales in every year of the Advance Design era, and conceptcarz states that Chevrolet trucks ranked first in US sales from 1947 through 1955. Neither gives a unit count. Cars.com's history of Chevrolet pickups puts the company's car-to-truck production ratio at about 4 to 1 before the new trucks and closer to 2.5 to 1 by 1950, which describes how much the truck side grew but is not a pickup figure either. Wikipedia lists production as 1947 to 1955 with no volumes. The Classic Industries identification guide gives serial number prefixes (EP for half-ton, ER for three-quarter-ton, ES for one-ton on 1947 trucks), which identify a truck but are not production counts. On price, the only figure found is a NADA number quoted by HotCars: an original MSRP of about $1,419 for a 3100, given in an article about the 1954 truck and not tied to a body or cab. No dealer price sheet or Chevrolet price list was retrieved, so no verified US list price by model year is stated here, and the $1,419 figure is carried at low confidence. The start date is also soft: Cars.com says early 1947, while Jim Carter Truck Parts says the new trucks came out in mid-year after a run of carryover pre-war trucks. A reader with a Chevrolet truck production table from a published reference is the person who can close this gap.",
